            %p Verifying the details is straightforward.
          %h2#graphical_language Graphical language
          %p
            Rigid categories are best expressed using the language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/string-naive.png", :width => "300px"}/
          %p
            Even better, we often fix graphical notation for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext ev
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{ev}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext coev
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{coev}
            which makes these axioms even simpler to write out:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/string-notation-rigidity.png", :width => "300px"}/
          %p We can now express rigidity as follows:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/string-better.png", :width => "300px"}/
          %p
            Rigidity is thus graphically the statement that string diagrams can be “straightened out”. This approach is taken, for example, in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Selinger2011"} Selinger
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %p
            Note that this definition only asserts the existence of the dual objects. It does
            %em not
            assert that specific duals have been chosen. However, the choice of duals is unique up to unique isomorphism, justifying reference to ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the"}> the
            ’ dual of an object; in fact, this extends to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^*\colon M \to M
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            to pick duals for every object at once, we can make this into a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functor"} strict functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
